# Azure Networking Deep Dive

## 1. The Housing Society Analogy

To make Azure networking concepts stick, I map them to a **Secure Housing Society** built by a real estate developer (John):

| Housing Society Element | Description |
|---|---|
| Private land | The bounded, secured property John purchases |
| Blocks (Block 1, 2, 3) | Separate sections within the land, each with its own purpose and size |
| Houses within each block | Individual units that residents occupy |
| Compound wall + main gate + guard | Perimeter security — controls who enters the entire property |
| Block-level guard | Each block has its own guard; validates that visitors are authorized for *that* block |
| Individual house security | Optional extra guard hired by a specific homeowner |
| Paths + signboards inside blocks | Internal directions that help guests navigate to the right house |
| Property maintenance charges | The ongoing cost John bills residents for using the land and facilities |

---

## 2. Mapping the Analogy to Azure Networking

| Housing Society | Azure Equivalent |
|---|---|
| Private land | **Virtual Network (VNet)** |
| Blocks | **Subnets** |
| Houses / units | **VM Instances** |
| Compound wall + main gate + guard | **Azure Firewall** |
| Block-level guard | **Network Security Group (NSG)** |
| Individual house guard | **NSG applied at NIC/VM level** |
| Paths + signboards inside block | **Route Table + System Routes** |
| Domain name / address of property | **Azure DNS** |
| Delivery person routing traffic to correct block | **Load Balancer (App Gateway / Azure LB)** |
| Maintenance charges | **Azure Billing** |

---

## 3. Three-Tier Application Architecture on Azure

A typical enterprise application has three tiers, each placed in its own subnet:

```
VNet: 10.0.0.0/16
│
├── App Gateway Subnet  →  Load Balancer (public-facing)
├── Subnet-Web          →  Frontend / Web Application instances
├── Subnet-App          →  Backend / Business Logic instances
└── Subnet-DB           →  Database instances (most restricted)
```

- **Web subnet** — hosts frontend applications (login page, transaction page, logout page, etc.)
- **App subnet** — hosts backend services, one-to-one mapped with frontend apps
- **DB subnet** — hosts databases; never directly accessible from the internet

---

## 4. Availability Zones & High Availability

- A VNet requested in a region (e.g., East US) is automatically available across **Zone 1, Zone 2, and Zone 3** of that region.
- If a single availability zone goes down, the entire application in that zone becomes unavailable.

**Best practice — deploy at least two copies of every critical instance:**

```
Subnet-Web
├── Login App — Copy 1  (Availability Zone 1)
└── Login App — Copy 2  (Availability Zone 2)
```

- This ensures that if one zone fails, the other copy continues to serve traffic.
- The **load balancer** decides which copy receives each incoming request.

---

## 5. Azure Firewall

- Acts as the **perimeter security** for the entire VNet — equivalent to the compound wall, main gate, and head guard in the housing analogy.
- Sits at the boundary between the public internet and the VNet.
- Inspects all incoming traffic and blocks unauthorized or malicious requests before they reach any subnet or instance.
- Every request from the internet must pass through Azure Firewall first.

---

## 6. Load Balancers — App Gateway vs Azure Load Balancer

Azure has two main load balancers, used at different layers of the architecture.

### App Gateway (L7 — Application Layer Load Balancer)

- Placed in the **App Gateway Subnet**, in front of the web application subnet.
- Operates at **Layer 7 (HTTP/HTTPS)** — it can read the URL path and hostname.
- Makes routing decisions based on the **URL path**:

```
User hits: abhishek.com/login     → routed to Login App pool
User hits: abhishek.com/logout    → routed to Logout App pool
User hits: abhishek.com/payment   → routed to Payment App pool
```

- Within each pool it also balances load between Copy 1 and Copy 2 of that app.
- **Use when:** traffic is HTTP/HTTPS and routing needs to be path-based or host-based.

### Azure Load Balancer (L4 — Transport Layer Load Balancer)

- Placed in front of the **backend / app subnet**.
- Operates at **Layer 4 (TCP/UDP)** — does not inspect HTTP paths; makes routing decisions purely based on IP and port.
- Used for internal traffic flowing **from the web tier to the app tier**.
- Since the L7 load balancer has already determined which backend service to target, the L4 load balancer just needs to distribute load between the two copies of that backend service — fast and efficient.
- **Use when:** internal service-to-service traffic where HTTP-level routing is not needed.

### Summary

| Feature | App Gateway | Azure Load Balancer |
|---|---|---|
| OSI Layer | L7 (Application) | L4 (Transport) |
| Routing basis | URL path, hostname | IP address, port |
| Position | In front of web subnet | In front of app subnet |
| Traffic type | Public HTTP/HTTPS | Internal TCP/UDP |
| Use case | Path-based routing for web apps | Internal load distribution |

---

## 7. Azure DNS

- When a load balancer is created, Azure assigns it an **IP address** (e.g., `3.4.5.6`).
- Users never access websites by IP address — they use a **domain name** (e.g., `abhishek.com`).
- **Azure DNS** is the service where I purchase and manage domain names and map them to IP addresses via **DNS records**.

**How name resolution works:**

```
User types: abhishek.com
     ↓
Browser → Home Router → ISP DNS Server
     ↓
ISP DNS queries Azure DNS
     ↓
Azure DNS returns Load Balancer IP: 3.4.5.6
     ↓
Request sent to 3.4.5.6 (the Load Balancer)
```

- This is identical to searching for a property name on Google Maps — the name gets resolved to GPS coordinates (IP address) that guide you to the destination.

---

## 8. How a Full Request Flows

```
User: abhishek.com/login
         │
         ▼
  [ISP DNS] ──resolves──► [Azure DNS] ──returns IP──► 3.4.5.6
         │
         ▼
  [Azure Firewall]  ←── perimeter security check
         │ (allowed)
         ▼
  [App Gateway / L7 LB]  ←── routes based on /login path
         │
         ▼
  [Subnet-Web NSG]  ←── subnet-level security check
         │ (allowed)
         ▼
  [Login Web App — Copy 1 or Copy 2]
         │
         ▼
  [Azure Load Balancer / L4 LB]  ←── forwards to backend
         │
         ▼
  [Subnet-App NSG]  ←── subnet-level security check
         │ (allowed)
         ▼
  [Login Backend App — Copy 1 or Copy 2]
         │
         ▼
  [Subnet-DB NSG]  ←── only app subnet allowed
         │ (allowed)
         ▼
  [Database]
```

---

## 9. VNet Peering

- By default, two separate VNets **cannot communicate** with each other — they are fully isolated.
- **VNet Peering** allows two VNets to communicate by explicitly linking them.

**When is it needed?**

> Example: The `Nike-Sales` VNet needs to access a storage resource inside the `Nike-Billing` VNet.

**How it works:**

- I must have **Owner/Admin access** on both VNets.
- Modify the **route tables** of both VNets to add routing paths that allow traffic between them.
- Once peered, resources in one VNet can communicate with resources in the other as if they were on the same network.

> There are constraints around overlapping CIDR ranges — two VNets cannot be peered if their IP ranges overlap.

---

## 10. VNet Gateway

- **VNet Gateway** is an alternative to VNet Peering for connecting two virtual networks.
- Both VNet Peering and VNet Gateway achieve the same goal: enabling communication between two VNets.
- The choice between them depends on factors like latency, cost, and cross-region requirements — this will be covered in detail during hands-on sessions.

| Feature | VNet Peering | VNet Gateway |
|---|---|---|
| Latency | Low (direct link) | Slightly higher |
| Cost | Lower | Higher |
| Cross-region | Supported (Global Peering) | Supported |
| Typical use | Same-region VNet communication | Complex routing / transit scenarios |

---

## 11. VPN Gateway

- Used to connect an **on-premises / organizational network** to an **Azure VNet**.
- Relevant in **hybrid cloud** scenarios where some resources remain on-premises (e.g., a private data center) and others are in Azure.
- Establishes an encrypted tunnel between the on-premises network and the Azure VNet so both can communicate securely.

**Use case:**

```
Organization's On-Premises Data Center
          │
     [VPN Gateway]
          │  (encrypted tunnel)
     [Azure VNet]
          │
   Azure resources
```

---

## 12. Full Architecture Summary

```
Internet
   │
[Azure DNS] — resolves domain name to Load Balancer IP
   │
[Azure Firewall] — perimeter security
   │
[App Gateway Subnet]
   └── App Gateway (L7 LB) — path-based routing
          │
[Subnet-Web] ── NSG ── Route Table
   ├── Login App (Zone 1) + (Zone 2)
   ├── Logout App (Zone 1) + (Zone 2)
   └── Payment App (Zone 1) + (Zone 2)
          │
[Azure Load Balancer (L4 LB)] — internal load balancing
          │
[Subnet-App] ── NSG ── Route Table
   ├── Login Backend (Zone 1) + (Zone 2)
   └── Payment Backend (Zone 1) + (Zone 2)
          │
[Subnet-DB] ── NSG (only Subnet-App allowed)
   ├── Primary Database (Active)
   └── Replica Database (Passive)
```

---

## 13. Analogy Cheat Sheet

| Concept | Housing Society | Azure |
|---|---|---|
| Secured boundary | Private land | VNet |
| Sections within land | Blocks | Subnets |
| Individual units | Houses | VM Instances |
| Perimeter security | Compound wall + main gate + head guard | Azure Firewall |
| Section-level guard | Block guard | NSG (subnet level) |
| Individual unit guard | Personal guard | NSG (NIC/VM level) |
| Internal navigation | Paths + signboards | Route Table + System Routes |
| Property address lookup | Google Maps search | Azure DNS |
| Traffic director to correct block | Reception / concierge | App Gateway (L7) |
| Internal block-to-block routing | Internal courier | Azure Load Balancer (L4) |
| Connecting two societies | Inter-society gate agreement | VNet Peering / VNet Gateway |
| Office connected to society | Private road to office | VPN Gateway |
| Maintenance bill | Monthly charges | Azure Billing |

